func=jag::Client::MainLogic	func_addr=0x00025c20	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::Client::MainLogic(Client*, bool isLastTickOfFrame) - CERTAIN.\nPer-logic-tick entry, invoked from jag::GraphicsApp::Frame (0x00637670) via Client vtable slot\n+0xF0 as vt[+0xF0](this, i \e\e ticksThisFrame-1); the frame driver bumps clientCycle (+0x528) and\nrenderCycle (+0x530) around the call.\nEvidence (3 independent):\n 1. Call shape from the frame loop: (this, bool lastTick) - matches the 2-arg (client, byte) form\n    of the Linux jag::Client::MainLogic.\n 2. Head is a structural 1:1 with the Linux MainLogic head: leading "if (byte at high Client\n    offset) vt(this,0)", then a 5000 ms periodic process-memory poll, then a mutex-guarded bool\n    that early-outs into handler(connMgrLikeObj, clientCycle, renderCycle, lastTick).\n 3. That early-out call here is FUN_0010eaf0(client[0x198E0], *(int*)(client+0x528),\n    *(int*)(client+0x530), lastTick) - identical argument tuple to the Linux\n    LoginProtocolHandler(client[MAINLOGIC_MANAGER], clientCycle, renderCycle, lastTick).\nNote: the bulk of the tick body that GCC inlined into the Linux MainLogic stays outlined on MSVC\n(FUN_00025990 state query, FUN_00024fc0), so this function is far smaller than its Linux twin.
