func=jag::opcode::get_npc_stat	func_addr=0x00204570	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::opcode::get_npc_stat  (CS2 opcode; error path  references "get_npc_stat" ).\nPops a stat index from the ClientScriptState int stack (base +0x100, sp +0x10a0), validates the\nactive NPC via Entity::ENTITY_TYPE ([npc+0x10]\e\e1), then pushes current then max:\n  ECX \e [npc + idx*4 + 0x1140]   NPC current-stat array base\n  R8D \e [npc + idx*4 + 0x115c]   NPC max-stat array base\nBoth arrays are 7 dwords. Stat index 3 \e hitpoints, so ONPC.CURRENT_HP\e0x114c, ONPC.MAX_HP\e0x1168.\nLinux 949-4 counterpart jag::opcode::get_npc_stat  uses 0x1120 / 0x113c \e> Windows is +0x20,\nmatching the rest of the ONPC tail block.\nWARNING: the offset table's ONPC.MODEL_OVERRIDE_A\e0x1140 / MODEL_OVERRIDE_B\e0x115c collide with these\ntwo stat arrays and are almost certainly a misidentification.
func=jag::opcode::get_npc_stat	func_addr=0x00204570	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=38	cu_index_from_entry=7	text=NPC stat arrays, NOT model overrides. RCX \e jag::game::NPC (from scriptState+0xc3b8), RAX \e stat index popped off the CS2 int stack.\n  npc + idx*4 + 0x1140 \e CURRENT stat array, int[7]\n  npc + idx*4 + 0x115c \e MAX stat array,     int[7]\nThe two blocks are adjacent and exactly 7*4 \e 0x1c apart, and the NPC ctor zero-fills 0x1140..0x1177 as one region.\nCORRECTION: the offset table's ONPC.MODEL_OVERRIDE_A (0x1140) / MODEL_OVERRIDE_B (0x115c) name these same two arrays. They are misnamed - they are the current/max stat arrays. ONPC.CURRENT_HP (0x114c) and ONPC.MAX_HP (0x1168) are element 3 of each, i.e. the same memory.\nWriter: the NPC extended-info processor stores (index byte, 24-bit current, 24-bit max) into the same pair.
func=jag::ClientProt::Construct	func_addr=0x00111090	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::ClientProt::Construct(this, client) -- ctor of the ClientProt sender aggregate. Allocated 0x2870\nbytes and stored at client+0x19870, then pushed into the sender-provider list at client+0x19818.\n\nBase subobjects (each a MouseProtSenderBase-derived prot sender with vtable, client at +8,\nlastSentX/lastSentY at +0x10/+0x14, lastMoveSampleTime at +0x18, lastClickSampleTime at +0x20):\n  this+0x00  aggregate vtable 0x00b5a410\n  this+0x10  EVENT mouse sender, vtable 0x00b5a3e0 (slot0 EventMouseClickPending, slot1\n             SendEventMouseClick); its rings live on MainLogicManager, not here\n  this+0x38  NATIVE mouse sender, vtable 0x00b5a3b0 (slot0 NativeMouseClickPending, slot1\n             SendNativeMouseClick, slot2 prot descriptor, slot4 PopNativeMouseMoveSample,\n             slot5 WriteNativeMouseMoveSourceByte) -- type NativeMouseProt, owns both source rings\n\nRing initialisation proves the NativeMouseProt layout: the 10-iteration loop and the 200-iteration loop\nboth stride 0x30 and both zero sample+0x04..+0x13 and +0x28..+0x2f while seeding sample+0x20 \e 1;\nthe 8-byte stores at nativeProt+0x288 and +0x2810 zero each ring's write/read cursor pair.\n\nRegisters the NativeMouseProt's two listeners into the client's InputHandler (client+0x18E50):\n  clickListener (this+0x38+0x68) -> client+0x19678  (native-mouse-BUTTON listener vector, +0x828)\n  moveListener  (this+0x38+0x28) -> client+0x196e0  (native-mouse-MOVE   listener vector, +0x890)\nEach is a 0x40-byte eastl::function whose +0x38 self-pointer is what DispatchMouseEventWithSource\ndereferences before calling invoker slot +0x10. Torn down by\njag::ClientProt::DestroyNativeMouseProt.
func=jag::MiniMenu::MiniMenu	func_addr=0x0012c2f0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::MiniMenu constructor. Builds the per-family action arrays, then calls the only two DoAction\nregistration functions in the binary:\n  jag::MiniMenu::RegisterComponentDoActions   -  7 actions (interface-component family)\n  jag::MiniMenu::RegisterDoActions            - 59 actions\n\e> 66 DoActions total for this build. NOTE: the inline list in RegisterDoActions' own comment is\nlabelled "61 entries" but actually enumerates 59; 59 is correct, 61 is a miscount.\n\nThe arrays built here are what fix the canonical op ordering, independent of the registration order:\n  loc array          c6aae0 c6ab40 c6aba0 c6ac00 c6ac60 c6acc0 -> ids 2 3 4 5 1001 1002 \e OBJECT_1..6\n  ground-item array  c6b210 c6b260 c6b2b0 c6b300 c6b350 c6b3a0 -> ids 18 19 20 21 22 1004 \e GROUND_ITEM_1..6\n  player array       c6b3f0..c6b6c0 then c6b710..c6b9e0       -> ids 44..53 then 2044..2053\nThis is what proves OBJECT_5/OBJECT_6 are ids 1001/1002 and NOT the alias ids 6/7, which merely\nreuse the same senders.
func=jag::GraphicsApp::Frame	func_addr=0x00637680	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::GraphicsApp::Frame(App* this, ? renderArg) - PROBABLE (role CERTAIN, class PROBABLE).\nPer-frame driver on the app base class shared by jag::Client and the --compileshader sibling app.\nComputes how many logic ticks are owed this frame, then runs\n  for (i \e 0; i < ticks; i++) { vt[+0xF0](this, i \e\e ticks-1);   // jag::Client::MainLogic\n                                ++*(int*)(this+0x568);\n                                ++*(int*)(this+0x528);           // clientCycle\n                                ++*(int*)(this+0x52C); }\nand afterwards vt[+0xF8](this, renderArg) followed by ++*(int*)(this+0x530) (renderCycle).\nThis is where CLIENT_CYCLE (+0x528) and CLIENT_RENDER_CYCLE (+0x530) are established: 0x528 is\nbumped once per logic tick, 0x530 once per rendered frame, and jag::Client::MainLogic forwards both\nas the (int,int) pair to the tick handler at 0x0010eaf0.\nClass name taken from the only Jagex app class present in the MSVC RTTI set\n(".?AV?$_Binder@...P8GraphicsApp@jag@@EAA_NXZPEAV34@@std@@"); not independently confirmed.
func=jag::game::InterfaceList::GetStaticComponent	func_addr=0x00349a20	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::game::InterfaceList::GetStaticComponent — Windows counterpart of Linux 0x593010. Packed-id lookup: interface \e id>>16, component \e id&0xffff.\n  0x349a51 MOV R9,[RAX+0x8]            -> InterfaceList entry +0x8 \e InterfaceParent*   (OInterfaceList.PARENT_OFFSET \e 0x8)\n  0x349a5a MOV RAX,[R9+0x8]            -> InterfaceParent +0x8 \e child-slot array BEGIN (OInterfaceParent.CHILD_ARRAY_BEGIN \e 0x8)\n  0x349a61..69 idx*0x18 + 0x10         -> stride 0x18, component shared_ptr at slot+0x10\nAll identical to Linux.
