func=jag::packethandlers::Loc::LocAddChange	func_addr=0x000edec0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=ServerProt loc-add handler; queues a LocChangeRequest with kind \e 1. Wire: [g4 middle-endian packed coord: plane\e(v&gt;&gt;0x1c)&amp;3, tileX\e(v&gt;&gt;0xe)&amp;0x3fff, tileY\ev&amp;0x3fff, 0xffffffff \e none][g4 middle-endian typeId][g1][g1 add-transformed: rotation \e v&amp;3, shape \e (v&gt;&gt;2)&amp;0x1f, high bit selects an extended float block].\nThe request is a pooled object; its shared_ptr is {control \e obj, record \e obj+0x20}, so all LocChangeRequest field offsets below are relative to obj+0x20 - which is what IsSupersededBy reads via *(shared_ptr+8). Record: +0x8 plane, +0xc tileX, +0x10 tileY, +0x14 shape, +0x15 rotation, +0x48 bool (shape bucket non-zero), +0x60 typeId, +0x64 the second wire byte, +0x68 kind (1 here), +0x70 cycle. Pending list is Client+0x198c8.
func=jag::game::ServerConnection::FlushClientMessages	func_addr=0x002edb40	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=ServerConnection outgoing flush. bool Flush(ServerConnection* conn).\n\nDrains the pending-message intrusive list at conn+0x48/+0x50, and for each message calls\n  jag::ClientStream::Write(conn->stream /* +0x08 */, msg->bufData /* +0x18 */, msg->payloadLen /* +0x28 */)\nthen jag::ClientStream::Flush(conn->stream) once the queue empties.\n\nCONFIRMS (Windows-only evidence, no Linux import):\n  ServerConnection +0x08 \e ClientStream*  -- passed as `this` to Write/Flush here, and as `this`\n      to ClientStream::Read/GetAvailable in ReadIncomingMessage ; ProcessConnections\n       guards on **(int**)(conn+8) \e\e 2. Three independent functions.\n  TcpConnectionMessage +0x18 \e buffer data ptr (the `src` arg of Write)\n  TcpConnectionMessage +0x28 \e payload byte count (the `len` arg of Write)\n\nOther ServerConnection fields used here: +0x58 freelist head, +0x60/+0x68 pool bump/end,\n+0x70 node stride, +0x80 pool block base, +0x88 pending count, +0x2b0 pending bytes,\n+0x2e4 lifetime bytes-sent counter.\nHYPOTHESIS: no official symbol recovered - do not rename on this evidence alone.
func=jag::input::Input::OnKeyDownInner	func_addr=0x00033dc0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::input::Input::OnKeyDownInner(this, vkCode). Virtual, Input vtable  slot +0x08.\n`this` \e\e jag::Client + 0x18d80 (Input is embedded, as on Linux).\nLocks this+0x18, walks the named-handler rbtree this+0x70 -> anchor this+0x68 calling\nFUN_007896f0(node+0x38, vk) and stopping on the first handler that consumes, then falls through to\nthe global handler at this+0x08. Finally sets the pressed byte in the InputState key-down map.\nInputState key-down map (this is the structure Linux IsKeyDown probes):\n    anchor DAT_00c78400, root DAT_00c78410; node key \e int at node+0x20, pressed byte at node+0x24.\nMissing keys are inserted via FUN_0078e5e0 before the byte is set.\nThe vk 0x1CA3 sentinel is the console toggle: the dispatch pre-fires it when the incoming vk\nequals this+0xb8 (CONSOLE_KEY_VK, initialised to 0xDF by the Client ctor at client+0x18e38).\nThat path checks vk 0x12 (VK_MENU) in the same key-down map and sets the console-open flag at\nthis+0xc8, which OnKeyCharInner then uses to swallow character input.\nEvidence: (1) reached from Input vtable slot +0x08, which the WndProc dispatch FUN_0078a2d0 calls\nfor WM_KEYDOWN/WM_SYSKEYDOWN; (2) touches exactly the Input fields established from OnMouseMotion -\nmutex +0x18, rbtree +0x68/+0x70 with the value at node+0x38, globalHandler +0x08; (3) does NOT call\njag::input::Input::OnMouseMotion (0x0078bb90) - the discriminator that exposed the touch-handler\nclass twice in earlier tranches.
