func=jag::hal::RenderView::HandleMessage	func_addr=0x00728730	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=RenderView message handler - reached from jag::hal::JagRenderViewProc  via\nGWLP_USERDATA. Handles WM_CREATE (cursor load), WM_SIZE (client-rect cache + min/max state) and\nWM_SETCURSOR itself, then delegates EVERYTHING ELSE - all keyboard and mouse messages - to\n    FUN_0078a2d0(*(jag::input::Input**)(this + 0x10), hwnd, msg, wParam, lParam)\nfalling through to DefWindowProcA when that returns false.\nRenderView layout: +0x10 jag::input::Input* <-- THE INPUT OBJECT\n  +0x18/+0x19/+0x1a maximised/minimised/restore state bytes   +0x20 HWND\n  +0x28 pending cursor, +0x30 current cursor, +0x38 default arrow cursor, +0x40 cursor mutex\n  +0x90/+0x94/+0x98/+0x9c cached client rect (l/t/r/b)\nThe RenderView itself is the object the top-level frame holds at frame+0xe0 (see\njag::hal::JagWindowProc, which does SetFocus/MoveWindow on frame[0xe0]->[0x20]).\nNEXT STEP for the keyboard hooks: find the writer of RenderView+0x10 - it is created in the\nwindow thread FUN_00729ce0 - which gives the jag::input::Input constructor, hence its real vtable,\nhence slots +0x8/+0x10/+0x18 \e OnKeyDown/OnKeyUp/OnKeyChar.
func=jag::ClientProt::RecordNativeMouseMove	func_addr=0x001412e0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::ClientProt::RecordNativeMouseMove -- producer of the NATIVE movement ring, and the Win32-only\ncounterpart of the game-event producer\njag::MainLogicManager::MouseInputRecorder::OnMouseMove (see the Linux program, where that\ndelegate is named and fully documented).\n\nCADENCE: also once per OS mouse message, but on a DIFFERENT and LOWER-LEVEL path. It has exactly\none reference in the whole image, a DATA reference from a delegate/listener table, so it is\ninvoked as a bound callback, never called directly. Its argument list is the proof of which path:\nbesides x and y it receives the Win32 MESSAGE ID and a SOURCE-FLAGS word, neither of which the\ngame-event onMouseMove delegate has or could supply. So the two movement rings are fed by two\nindependent producers on two independent event streams; they are not two views of one path.\n\nIt early-returns false unless the connection is established, so nothing is recorded outside the\nin-game session. It writes the sample's button field to -1 (movement), x/y as floats, the global\nclock as the timestamp, then the message id and the source flags, and applies the same\ndrop-oldest wrap rule as the game ring. The source-flags word is what\njag::ClientProt::WriteNativeMouseMoveSourceByte later appends per history sample, one byte each.
