func=jag::ClientProt::PopEventMouseMoveSample	func_addr=0x00112220	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::ClientProt::PopEventMouseMoveSample -- vtable slot 4 of the EVENT mouse sender. Pops one sample\nfrom MainLogicManager's MOVEMENT ring and advances its read cursor, or returns NULL when empty.\n\nPROVES MainLogicManager owns TWO SEPARATE RINGS of identical MouseSample (0x28) layout, back to back:\n  CLICK ring     base +0x46f0, 50 slots  -> +0x46f0 + 50*0x28  \e +0x4ec0\n    write cursor +0x4ec0, read cursor +0x4ec4\n  MOVEMENT ring  base +0x4ec8, 200 slots -> +0x4ec8 + 200*0x28 \e +0x6e08\n    write cursor +0x6e08, read cursor +0x6e0c\nThis function computes its entry as base(+0x44c8) + (idx*5 + 0x140)*8 \e +0x4ec8 + idx*0x28 and wraps the\nread cursor at 199. Independently confirmed by jag::MainLogicManager::Construct, whose two init loops are\n50 x 0x28 from +0x46f0 and 200 x 0x28 from +0x4ec8, each followed by an 8-byte zero store clearing\nexactly that ring's cursor pair; and by jag::Client::DiscardPendingInput, which drains both rings by\ncopying write into read.\n\nThe two rings feed DIFFERENT ClientProts. Sender vtable slot 2 returns the movement-history prot\ndescriptor (EVENT: DAT_00e8f600; NATIVE: DAT_00e8fbe0), while each Send hardcodes its own click\ndescriptor (EVENT: DAT_00e8f5e0; NATIVE: DAT_00e8f6c0). Four distinct mouse prots in total. Slot 5 (the\nper-sample extra byte) is a bare `ret` for the EVENT sender, so the EVENT movement-history packet carries\nno per-sample extra; the NATIVE one appends one source-flags byte per sample.\n\nCROSS-CHECKED ON THE SHIPPING LINUX BUILD (rs2client.949-4, read-only): the EVENT sender's vtable at\n0x01419ba8 has the identical 6-slot shape, and its slot 4 at 0x00174b20 is the same pop -- 0x28 stride,\nread cursor at the write cursor's address + 4, wrap at 199 -- reading a cursor pair distinct from the one\nSendEventMouseClick uses for the click ring. GCC folded the base constants into relocation/string symbols\nso the literal displacements are not readable from that decompilation, but the two-ring design is present\nand unchanged on Linux. Cross-compiler byte sig-scan is NOT applicable between these two images.
