func=jag::opcode::SendComponentToFrontOrBack	func_addr=0x001f6c70	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::opcode::SendComponentToFrontOrBack — Windows counterpart of Linux 0x2f0d80 (matched on the shared "%s: component does not have a parent" / "SendComponentToFrontOrBack" string pair).\nInterfaceComponent field evidence (R9 \e componentRef[1], the component):\n  0x1f6cab CMP word ptr [R9+0xc],CX          -> SLOT_ID \e 0xc (0xffff \e none)\n  0x1f6cb6 MOVZX EAX,word ptr [R9+0xe]       -> ushort PARENT SLOT INDEX, 0xffff \e none. NOT a component type:\n           when !\e 0xffff the else-branch at 0x1f6d2f..0x1f6d3b resolves the parent as\n           *( *(InterfaceParent+0x8) + idx*0x18 + 0x10 ), i.e. it indexes the parent's child-slot array.\n  0x1f6cc0 MOV RDX,[R9+0x10] / 0x1f6cf3 MOV RDI,[R9+0x10] / 0x1f6cf7 MOV RSI,[R9+0x18]\n           -> PARENT_SHAREDPTR \e 0x10: shared_ptr<InterfaceComponent>{ refcounter @+0x10, pointee @+0x18 }.\n           RDI is IncRef/DecRef'd through +0x8/+0xc; RSI is the object the vtable +0x178/+0x180 call targets,\n           and the +0xe index path produces the SAME value from slot+0x10, proving both are the parent pointer.\nProves OInterfaceComponent.PARENT_SHAREDPTR \e 0x10 (identical to Linux) and re-proves CHILD_ARRAY_BEGIN\e0x8 / CHILD_SLOT_PTR\e0x10 / stride 0x18.
func=jag::MainLogicManager::Construct	func_addr=0x0010dd40	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::MainLogicManager::Construct(this, client) -- allocated 0x7898 bytes, stored at client+0x198e0.\n\nAuthoritative proof of the two mouse rings' geometry. Two initialisation loops, both stride 0x28 and\nboth zeroing sample+0x04..+0x13 while seeding sample+0x20 \e 1 (the same field pattern as\nNativeMouseSample):\n  loop 1: 50 iterations from this+0x4700  -> entry base this+0x46f0, ends at this+0x4ec0\n          followed by an 8-byte zero store at this+0x4ec0, clearing write(+0x4ec0)/read(+0x4ec4)\n  loop 2: 200 iterations from this+0x4ed8 -> entry base this+0x4ec8, ends at this+0x6e08\n          followed by an 8-byte zero store at this+0x6e08, clearing write(+0x6e08)/read(+0x6e0c)\nSo: CLICK ring \e 50 x MouseSample at +0x46f0; MOVEMENT ring \e 200 x MouseSample at +0x4ec8. Adjacent,\nindependent, identical entry layout.\n\nAlso registers three listener objects into the client's InputHandler (client+0x18E50) via the\nlistener-set insert at handler+0x980, and two callables into its own listener vectors. None of those\nregistrations is the ring producer -- the object at this+0x7090 carries vtable 0x00b5a690 whose\nnon-destructor slots are all input-module base-class defaults (touch/pointer), not ring pushes.
func=jag::game::DoOpLoc2	func_addr=0x0010b9e0	kind=plate	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Setting prototype: void DoOpObject2(void *callable, void *entry)
func=jag::opcode::cam_forceangle	func_addr=0x00095990	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=CS2 opcode CAM_FORCEANGLE(int xAngle, int yAngle).\nPops 2 ints off the ClientScriptState int stack (base +0x100, sp +0x10a0, sp -\e 2):\n  slot[sp]   \e xAngle (pitch)   slot[sp+1] \e yAngle (yaw)\nGuards on Client+0x19f68 (LOGGED_IN_PLAYER) !\e 0, then loads the camera from\nClient+0x19858 (OClient.CAMERA) and tail-calls jag::game::Camera::ForceAngle(cam, pitch, yaw).\nReturns the CS2 success constant DAT_00c69670 on success, DAT_00c696e0 otherwise.\n\nEvidence:\n - Camera::ForceAngle has exactly two callers: this function and\n   jag::PacketHandlers::CamForceangle (the CAM_FORCEANGLE ServerProt handler), so the\n   opcode identity is unambiguous.\n - Camera::ForceAngle clamps arg1 into [0x80,0x180] and wraps arg2 modulo 0x800 \e>\n   arg1 is the pitch/x angle (RS3 camera pitch range) and arg2 the 0..2047 yaw/y angle.\n - cs2-dumps: CAM_FORCEANGLE(int,int) is the only 2-int camera-angle setter; call sites\n   pass CAM_GETANGLE_XA() first and a 0..2047 value second, e.g. CAM_FORCEANGLE(268, 644).\n - Linux 949-5 jag::PacketHandlers::CamForceangle has the identical downstream shape.
