func=jag::ClientStream::Close	func_addr=0x0063c510	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::ClientStream::Close(socket) - `this` here is the nested socket sub-object at ClientStream+0x8.\nClears the eastl::string at +0x20 (SSO marker byte at +0x27 restored to 0x17), zeroes the u16 at\n+0x48, sets the fd/SOCKET at +0x0 to -1 and calls ws2_32 closesocket (Ordinal_3).\nEvidence: identical field set and identical ordering to Linux jag::ClientStream::Close (which ends\nin close(fd) instead of closesocket), and it is the close used by Read/Write/Flush/GetAvailable.
func=jag::input::Input::OnMiddleButtonUp	func_addr=0x0078b820	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Input::OnMiddleButtonUp(Input* this, float x, float y) - name derived by analogy, no reference symbol.\nReached from FUN_0078a2f0 case 0x208 (WM_MBUTTONUP) at 0x0078a692.\nClears MIDDLE_BUTTON_STATE (0x00e8e4f5) \e 0 and walks the MMOUSE_UP slot inline:\nbegin handler+0x140, end +0x148, mutex +0x158. Proves OInputHandler.MMOUSE_UP \e 0x140.\nThe matching middle-DOWN path is NOT a separate function - it is open-coded in FUN_0078a2f0\ncase 0x207 at 0x0078a5b0 (sets 0x00e8e4f5 \e 1, slot begin handler+0xd8 / end +0xe0 / mutex +0xf0),\nwhich independently re-confirms OInputHandler.MMOUSE_DOWN \e 0xd8.
